## First — what that line does

```c
TxData = (uint32_t)0x2322AF24u;
(void)SPORT0_DMATransmitDataBuffer(&TxData, 0x00u);
```

This is the **"test stopped" ACK packet** sent back to the GUI over the SPORT serial link.

- `TxData` is a 32-bit variable that holds whatever word you want to transmit next.
- `0x2322AF24u` is the specific hex code for "Step Test Stop acknowledged." The byte breakdown is: `0x23` = packet footer/stop marker, `0x22` = command subcode for Stop, `0xAF24` = packet-trailer / unit identifier for the ACU card.
- `SPORT0_DMATransmitDataBuffer(&TxData, 0x00u)` tells the SPORT0 peripheral: "DMA this one 32-bit word out, no additional words." The `0x00` is the extra-byte count.

Compare the matching start-ACK you already have in `StepTest_Process`:

```c
TxData = (uint32_t)0x2311AF24u;    /* 0x11 = Start subcode */
(void)SPORT0_DMATransmitDataBuffer(&TxData, 0x00u);
```

Same transmit mechanism, different subcode byte (`0x11` for Start, `0x22` for Stop). When the GUI sees `0x2322AF24` arrive, it knows the ACU has formally ended the test and it can grey out the Stop button, close the file writer, etc.

The identical ACK is sent from the switch case when the GUI's own Stop button is pressed:
```c
case STEP_TEST_STOP :
    TestInProgress = 0u;
    TxData = (uint32_t)0x2322AF24u;         /* <-- same code */
    (void)SPORT0_DMATransmitDataBuffer(&TxData, 0x00u);
    MotorStepTestStop();
    break;
```

So our auto-stop sends the exact same message the GUI's Stop button would — the GUI can't tell the difference.

## Now — why the motor keeps stopping at ~225 ms

Look at your symptoms across every single run:

| Attempt | What we tried | Where motor stopped |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | TimeValue + 0.0005, bug (reset to 0) | ~220 ms |
| 2 | Fixed TimeValue, with reset | ~220 ms |
| 3 | Same, different run | ~235 ms |
| 4 (first) | Rare case where it worked | 520 ms ✓ |
| 4 (second) | Same build, same session | 155 ms |
| 5 | Changed target to 100 ms | 2030 ms |
| Latest | Gate restored + TimeValue | 225 ms |

The stop point is **unrelated to our target**. It bunches around 200–240 ms regardless of whether we ask for 100 ms, 520 ms, or anything else. If our auto-stop block were firing, the time would at least move when we change `0.52f`. It doesn't.

**Our auto-stop code is almost certainly never firing in the ~225 ms cases.** Something else is ending the test (or shutting the motor off). The most likely culprit — and I should have flagged this harder in the earlier replies — is this line inside `MotorTwoLoopPID_ControlAlgo` in `ACU_PID_Algo.c`:

```c
volt_sat = MAX_VOLT;    // change to determine TF of CAS 230926
```

This line **overwrites the computed PID output and forces full voltage every single tick**, in whatever direction the error sign says. The motor is not being position-controlled at all. It's being slammed open-loop at 100 % PWM.

What that does on the Faulhaber mini-motor:
1. 100 % PWM applied → motor accelerates hard toward target.
2. Current spikes because there's no velocity-loop limiting.
3. Motor hits mechanical stop (or near it) at ~200 ms — the time matches the mechanical travel of a small DC motor at full voltage.
4. Driver's over-current / stall protection trips → motor is cut off → the data stream stops because the FPGA stops reporting fresh positions, or the driver fault pulls `TestInProgress` down through a fault path, or your scope just stops showing motion.

The occasional "it worked and stopped at 520" was probably a run where the stall didn't trip (lower current, cooler driver, slight slack in the mechanism).

## The actual fix — one line in `ACU_PID_Algo.c`

Open `ACU_PID_Algo.c`, find this block (it's in `MotorTwoLoopPID_ControlAlgo`, right after the two existing `volt_sat` clamps):

```c
if(volt_op > MAX_VOLT)
{
    volt_sat = MAX_VOLT;
}
if(volt_op < (-MAX_VOLT))
{
    volt_sat = (-MAX_VOLT);
}

volt_sat = MAX_VOLT;    // change to determine TF of CAS 230926   <-- THIS LINE
```

**Comment out that last `volt_sat = MAX_VOLT;` line.** Make it:

```c
/* volt_sat = MAX_VOLT;    // change to determine TF of CAS 230926 */
```

That line was added by someone (the comment says `230926` — likely 26 Sept 2023) to measure the control-system transfer function with full-step input. It is **not meant for normal operation**. With it active, the PID calculation that happens above it is completely thrown away.

With it commented out:
- PID will properly command velocity → velocity loop will properly command voltage → PWM will be whatever duty the controller actually wants (small near the setpoint, larger when far away).
- Motor will move toward the target smoothly and settle, instead of slamming into it.
- No stall, no current trip, no mysterious stop at 225 ms.
- Your 520 ms auto-stop (either `TimeValue > 0.52f` or `Std_timestamp >= 520u`) will fire cleanly.

## Order of operations

1. **First**, keep your current Step-Test auto-stop code and the gate restored as in my previous message. Don't change those.
2. Open `ACU_PID_Algo.c`, comment out the single `volt_sat = MAX_VOLT;` line that has the `230926` comment on it. **Do not touch the two `volt_sat = MAX_VOLT;` lines inside the `if(volt_op > ...)` and `if(volt_op < ...)` blocks above it — those are the proper saturation clamps and must stay.** Only the standalone, unconditional one at the end is the problem.
3. Rebuild, flash, power-cycle, run Step Test.

